select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-b06-009","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"Why must a company designate an energy manager, and where should that person report?","back":"If no one has energy management as a specific part of their job, it gets lower priority than other duties. The energy manager should report as high as possible in the organisation without losing line orientation.","explain":"The energy manager should be strong, dynamic, goal-oriented and a good manager, supported with resources including staff. In BEE's textile mill example, the energy manager reports to the factory manager, alongside the production, utilities/maintenance and finance managers.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p167","order":1281},
{"id":"p1-b06-010","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"Name five responsibilities of an energy manager listed by BEE.","back":"Any five of: prepare an annual activity plan of attractive investments; establish an energy conservation cell; improve monitoring and process control; analyse equipment efficiency; ensure instruments are working and calibrated; run internal workshops; disseminate consumption data to shop level; establish an SEC calculation method; manage training; build a knowledge bank; coordinate external audits and projects; network with other energy managers.","explain":"BEE separates responsibilities (a broad list of 15) from duties (5, linked to the EC Act). A long-answer question asks for at least 5 duties and 3 responsibilities.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p167","order":1282},
{"id":"p1-b06-011","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"List the five duties of an energy manager under BEE.","back":"1) Report to BEE and the State Designated Agency once a year on energy consumed and action taken on the accredited auditor's recommendations; 2) establish improved data recording, collection and analysis; 3) support the accredited energy audit firm; 4) provide information to BEE as demanded under the Act; 5) prepare and implement a scheme for efficient use of energy as per the EC Act regulations.","explain":"Trap: 'create a knowledge bank on sectoral and national energy efficiency developments' is a responsibility, not a duty. The book's MCQ uses exactly this distractor.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p168","order":1283},
{"id":"p1-b06-012","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What is one of the most important roles of the energy manager, and how can claimed savings be made credible?","back":"Marketing (selling) the energy management programme. Savings goals can be shown to be realistic with data and quotes from other companies, published in literature or obtained by contacting their energy managers.","explain":"Without internal marketing, the programme struggles to get resources and cooperation from departments.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p168","order":1284},
{"id":"p1-b06-013","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"Why do most successful energy management programmes have an energy committee, and who is on it?","back":"Not all the talent needed resides in one person or discipline. The committee has members with strong technical backgrounds (chemical, industrial, electrical, civil, mechanical engineers etc.) who give technical help to the energy manager and plant staff.","explain":"The energy manager may be full-time, but the committee usually works part-time and is called on as needed; in multi-plant organisations it may be full-time. A committee encourages sharing of ideas, gets agreement on cross-departmental projects and gives a stronger voice to top management than one energy manager.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p168","order":1285},
{"id":"p1-b06-014","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"How often does an energy committee normally meet, and what does it review?","back":"Normally monthly. It reviews monthly production and energy consumption against targets, budgets and previous months, and the status of conservation projects implemented or planned.","explain":"The frequency depends on how important energy costs are in the company's cost structure and what projects are under way.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p169","order":1286},
{"id":"p1-b06-015","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What are the BEE requirements for collecting energy data to build an energy profile?","back":"Account for all energy sources (electricity, steam, oil, gas, waste fuels), purchased or self-generated, in physical units and cost. Assemble at least two years of data, summarised monthly, using more recent data if available.","explain":"The data must be complete and accurate, since it is used for analysis and goal setting. It comes from utility bills and sub-meters, by measurement, calculation or estimation. Non-energy data (building size, production, operating hours) are also tracked to set Energy Performance Indicators.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p169","order":1287},
{"id":"p1-b06-016","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What is normalisation of energy data, and give normalisation factors for industry and for commercial buildings.","back":"Removing the impact of factors other than efficiency (season, occupancy, raw material quality etc.) so energy performance can be compared. Industry: inputs, product type, output, production process. Commercial buildings: climate zone, building area, energy choice, energy cost, occupancy, operating hours.","explain":"Exam trap: 'maintenance cost' is not a common normalising factor for industrial facilities.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p170","order":1288},
{"id":"p1-b06-017","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What is the minimum data period for an energy baseline, and what is preferable?","back":"A minimum of 12 months of data. An average over the past two to three years is more desirable.","explain":"The baseline is the reference point from which progress is measured. Identify energy performance indicators (e.g. kcal/ton, kcal/kWh, total energy cost/ton), and publish and share the baseline with managers and key stakeholders.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p170","order":1289},
{"id":"p1-b06-018","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish quantitative and qualitative reviews when analysing energy use patterns.","back":"Quantitative: find consumption peaks and valleys and link them to operations or events; compare the performance of similar facilities. Qualitative: interviews and informed opinions, lessons learned, in-house audits and surveys, and reviewing policies and operating procedures.","explain":"The analysis identifies significant energy uses, meaning those accounting for a high proportion of total consumption. The energy profile must be updated at set intervals and after major changes in facilities, equipment or processes.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p170","order":1290},
{"id":"p1-b06-019","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What are the components of energy planning in the BEE guide book?","back":"Developing an energy policy, setting objectives and targets, preparing detailed action plans, allocating management resources, and using management techniques (5S, TPM, TQM, Kaizen, ISO 50001) for implementation.","explain":"Planning rests on the premise that strategic goals are only achieved if they are driven by day-to-day actions.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p171","order":1291},
{"id":"p1-b06-020","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What two roles does a formal written energy policy play?","back":"1) A public expression of the organisation's commitment to energy management. 2) A working document that guides energy management practice and provides continuity.","explain":"The energy policy provides the foundation for setting performance goals and integrating energy management into the organisation's culture and operations. Ideally it is a formal written declaration with stated objectives, an action plan and clear responsibilities.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p171","order":1292},
{"id":"p1-b06-021","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"How should an energy policy be drafted and adopted, and who publishes it?","back":"All departmental representatives should be invited to contribute when it is first formulated and when it is reviewed. It should then be formally adopted and ratified by the head of the organisation; publishing the policy is a top-management role.","explain":"Wider participation gives wider acceptance. The policy usually states goals and concrete targets, often with major measures and timetables, and must match the company's mission statement or overall strategy.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p172","order":1293},
{"id":"p1-b06-022","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What first-year energy reduction goal does BEE consider adequate, and why are yearly goals usually better than multi-year goals?","back":"First-year goals of 3–6% reduction in energy consumption or SEC are adequate for most companies. Yearly goals let rewards be seen sooner, success be celebrated more often, and corrections be made more frequently, especially if production changes.","explain":"A good rule is to start with small goals. Goals must be measurable, time-bound and formally approved by senior management. They can be based on audit findings, technical or economic feasibility, or benchmarking. An example of a multi-year goal is 20% savings over 3 years.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p172","order":1294},
{"id":"p1-b06-023","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What does SMART mean for energy targets in the BEE guide book?","back":"Specific and Measurable; Ambitious (committing the organisation to continual improvement); Realistic, so they can be achieved within specified Time limits.","explain":"Targets are normally set at departmental level to achieve organisational goals, and should be attainable but challenging. They are expressed through energy performance indicators such as energy per item, per kg, per m² or %.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p173","order":1295},
{"id":"p1-b06-024","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"In BEE's ISO 50001 cement plant example, how do goal, objectives and targets differ?","back":"Goal: the broad policy aim (reduce energy per output by 15% vs 2015 by 2017). Objectives: steps towards it (cut coal use 10% by 2016; cut clinker SEC from 90 to 85 kWh/t). Targets: the specific measures (e.g. online O₂ analyser for kiln excess air, cooler waste heat recovery, waste tyres as fuel, efficient raw mill fan, VFD on dust collector fan).","explain":"Each target then gets an action plan with time, actions, responsibility and resources. In the example, the online O₂ analyser target has the General Manager overall responsible and a budget of Rs 5,00,000.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p186","order":1296},
{"id":"p1-b06-025","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What is internal benchmarking, and when is it used for setting targets?","back":"Using the company's own historical best performance as the basis for targets. It is used when external benchmarking is impractical because of confidentiality or lack of detailed comparable data.","explain":"External benchmarking needs very detailed data on energy and on processes, raw materials and products. Comparisons not made on the same basis give misleading results.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p174","order":1297},
{"id":"p1-b06-026","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"List the steps in creating an energy action plan.","back":"a) Evaluate audit reports and identify gaps between current and desired performance; b) list the activities needed to close each gap; c) list resources and time required; d) designate overall responsibility; e) facilitate budget negotiations and confirm budget availability.","explain":"Action plans are energy management programmes. To avoid duplicating resources, they should be built into normal business operations, and communicated to all departments involved (utilities, operations, HR, purchase, engineering, EHS etc.).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p174","order":1298},
{"id":"p1-b06-027","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"Classify each as a positive or negative force for 'reduce energy per unit of production': high energy price; competing corporate priorities; available efficient technology; lack of awareness; incentive for high power factor; insufficient finance.","back":"Positive: high energy price, efficient technology available, PF incentive. Negative: competing corporate priorities, lack of awareness, insufficient financial resources.","explain":"Other forces in BEE's Figure 6.4: positive are top management commitment and energy being a high share of product cost; negative are absence of a corporate energy policy and insufficient skills. The book's tip: it is usually more effective to minimise negative forces than to strengthen positive ones.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p175","order":1299},
{"id":"p1-b06-028","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"List the steps of force field analysis.","back":"1) State the goal and the direction towards it; 2) identify barriers (negative forces), internal or external; 3) identify positive forces; 4) estimate the relative strength of each (low, medium, high); 5) prioritise the forces the action plan can most effectively strengthen or weaken.","explain":"It is done before creating the action plan, to gain insight into the change process. External barriers can include energy rate structures or government regulation.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p174","order":1300},
{"id":"p1-b06-029","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What two major areas should employee training address in an energy management programme?","back":"1) Developing new skills in technologies (in-house or outside training). 2) Adopting new attitudes towards energy wastage and waste reduction.","explain":"Training is called the key to sustaining energy conservation. It must be continuous, not a one-off session, so it both informs and changes attitudes. Trained technical staff should in turn train operators in-house.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p176","order":1301},
{"id":"p1-b06-030","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"Give good practices for building energy awareness and motivation.","back":"Top management announces an energy policy and backs the team; incentives and recognition for successful projects; announcing results to all employees; participation at every step; divisional working groups; monthly evaluation of energy consumption and savings; in-house training; an annual energy conservation week with seminars, exhibitions and contests.","explain":"Communication methods include informal dialogue, work group meetings, posters, newsletters, bulletin boards and intranet sites. New employees get basic energy information during orientation.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p177","order":1302},
{"id":"p1-b06-031","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What features should an energy performance tracking (information) system have?","back":"A suitable frequency of data collection (daily, weekly or monthly); easy to use, update and maintain; used to communicate performance to all parts of the organisation to motivate change; formats understandable across the organisation that make reporting easy.","explain":"It can range from a simple spreadsheet to detailed databases and IT systems. Metering and sub-metering are needed for effective monitoring. Sharing results across departments promotes healthy competition.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p178","order":1303},
{"id":"p1-b06-032","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What are the levels and examples of internal recognition, and give an example of external recognition.","back":"Levels: individual, team (department or group) and facility. Types range from certificates and formal acknowledgements to salary increases, cash bonuses or shields, ideally given by senior management at an award ceremony. External: a National Energy Conservation Award.","explain":"Recognition criteria include best energy-saving idea, highest reduction in energy use, or savings increased by a quantified amount. External recognition validates the programme and enhances public image, making the organisation more attractive to customers, lenders and other stakeholders.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p178","order":1304},
{"id":"p1-b06-033","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"Name the five Japanese words of 5S and their English meanings.","back":"Seiri – housekeeping (separate needed from unneeded items); Seiton – workplace organisation (a place for everything); Seiso – cleanup; Seiketsu – cleanliness (constant state of readiness); Shitsuke – discipline.","explain":"5S is a simple but effective method of organising the workplace. Implementing it reduces hidden waste, improves quality and safety, and cuts lead time and cost.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p179","order":1305},
{"id":"p1-b06-034","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What do the letters PQCDS stand for as the benefits of 5S, and which cycle is used to implement 5S?","back":"P – increase Productivity; Q – improve product Quality; C – reduce manufacturing Costs; D – ensure on-time Delivery; S – provide a Safe working environment. Implementation follows the PDCA (Plan–Do–Check–Action) cycle.","explain":"Plan: organise a committee, plan each S, announce the programme. Do: training, a clean-up day, an organise-your-workplace day. Check: evaluate results. Action: self-examination and corrective action.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p180","order":1306},
{"id":"p1-b06-035","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What does KAIZEN mean, and what are its implementation steps?","back":"KAI = change, ZEN = good (for the better): continuous improvement involving everyone. Steps: 1) the operator identifies and writes down a problem; 2) the operator develops an improvement idea and goes to the supervisor; 3) the supervisor or Kaizen team reviews it, encourages action and fills the Kaizen form; 4) the idea is implemented and checked; 5) the operator is rewarded.","explain":"Kaizen events that target energy directly, such as steam or compressed air leak events where operators detect and fix leaks on the spot, give immediate returns. They also enrol employees in future conservation efforts.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p181","order":1307},
{"id":"p1-b06-036","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What is the principle of Small Group Activities (SGA) in energy management?","back":"Members discuss, think and judge based on facts and data rather than experience, intuition or gut feeling. Cross-functional shop-floor teams conceptualise, measure and implement energy projects.","explain":"This creates awareness and gives a sense of ownership and achievement. Plants using SGA developed a culture of energy savings and better sustained the savings. Planning runs from forming groups and defining roles, through setting targets and running meetings, to reporting and presenting the problems solved.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p182","order":1308},
{"id":"p1-b06-037","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"According to BEE, what share of energy costs can be saved by eliminating TPM's six big losses with proper maintenance, and what are the four strategies for integrating energy into TPM?","back":"An estimated 25% of energy costs. Strategies: integrate energy reduction into autonomous maintenance; train employees to identify energy wastes; conduct energy kaizen events; build energy best practices into safety, health and environment management systems.","explain":"TPM aims to maximise overall production system effectiveness and eliminate all accidents, defects and breakdowns, with everyone from top executive to shop floor taking part. Autonomous maintenance means operators maintain their own equipment: daily inspection, lubrication, parts replacement, simple repairs, abnormality detection and precision checks.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p183","order":1309},
{"id":"p1-b06-038","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"Give energy-reduction checklist items for combustion and steam generation systems from BEE's TPM section.","back":"Combustion: run furnaces and boilers near design capacity, reduce excess air, clean heat transfer surfaces, cut radiation losses from openings, insulate properly, install air preheat or heat recovery. Steam: better water treatment to minimise blowdown, optimise deaerator venting, repair leaks, minimise vented steam, maintain steam traps.","explain":"For process heating, the checklist adds: seal openings to stop air leakage, keep a slightly positive furnace pressure, reduce material-handling fixtures, and recover exhaust heat for lower-temperature processes.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p184","order":1310},
{"id":"p1-b06-039","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"Which phrase is associated with TQM, and why does TQM complement energy management?","back":"'Doing the right things right, first time'. Rejects waste the energy used to make them, e.g. a casting with blow holes must be scrapped.","explain":"TQM requires involvement of everyone from worker to top executive, visible management commitment, 'document what you do, then do what you documented', written and displayed mission and vision statements, people satisfaction as first priority, and emphasis on long-term needs.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p184","order":1311},
{"id":"p1-b06-040","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"What framework does ISO 50001:2011 require organisations to follow?","back":"Develop a policy for more efficient energy use; fix targets and objectives to meet the policy; use data to understand and decide on energy use; measure the results; review how well the policy works; continually improve energy management.","explain":"An EnMS addresses energy supply, measurement, documentation and reporting of energy use, and procurement and design practices for energy-using equipment. An exam MCQ answer is 'all of the above'.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p185","order":1312},
{"id":"p1-b06-041","paper":1,"topic":"Energy action planning","kind":"fact","front":"How does ISO 50001 relate to other ISO standards, and how much of world energy use could it influence?","back":"It uses the same continual-improvement management system model as ISO 9001 (quality) and ISO 14001 (environment), so it integrates easily with them. It could influence up to 60% of the world's energy use.","explain":"In ISO 50001:2011, clause 4.3 is the energy policy, 4.4 planning (energy review, legal requirements, objectives and targets), 4.5 implementation and operation, 4.6 checking performance, and 4.7 management review. The book summarises ISO 50001 as 'saying what you do and doing what you say', since action plans are documented and audited.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 06 p185","order":1313},
{"id":"p1-062","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What is simple payback period and what are its limitations?","back":"Simple payback = First cost (investment) ÷ Net annual cash savings. It does not account for the time value of money or for savings after the payback period.","explain":"It is quick and easy to understand, which makes it useful for screening low-cost energy measures. Two projects with the same payback can have very different lifetime value, so use NPV or IRR for larger investments.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q47","order":1314},
{"id":"p1-063","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"How is Return on Investment (ROI) calculated?","back":"ROI (%) = Annual net cash flow ÷ Capital cost × 100. Example: ₹20,000 per year on ₹1,00,000 → 20%.","explain":"ROI is the reciprocal of simple payback (×100). Like payback, it ignores the time value of money. For a project to be worthwhile, ROI should exceed the interest rate on borrowed money or the cost of capital.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q48","order":1315},
{"id":"p1-064","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"Define Net Present Value (NPV).","back":"NPV = Σ CFₜ ÷ (1 + i)ᵗ for t = 0 to n, the sum of all cash flows discounted to today at the discount rate i, with the investment entered as a negative cash flow at t = 0. Accept the project if NPV > 0.","explain":"The present value factor 1/(1+i)ᵗ converts future savings into today's money. NPV tells you how much value the project adds beyond the required return. Among mutually exclusive projects with no capital limit, choose the highest NPV.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q49","order":1316},
{"id":"p1-065","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"Define Internal Rate of Return (IRR).","back":"IRR is the discount rate at which the NPV of a project's cash flows equals zero. Accept the project if IRR > cost of capital.","explain":"IRR is found by trial and interpolation: IRR = r₁ + NPV₁ × (r₂ − r₁) ÷ (NPV₁ − NPV₂), where NPV₁ > 0 at the lower rate r₁ and NPV₂ < 0 at r₂. Both NPV and IRR account for the time value of money; simple payback and ROI do not.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q5, Q18","order":1317},
{"id":"p1-066","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"Two projects of equal life: X has IRR 40%, NPV ₹50,000; Y has IRR 30%, NPV ₹1,20,000. With no capital limit, which do you choose?","back":"Project Y, because it has the higher NPV.","explain":"When NPV and IRR rankings conflict for mutually exclusive projects and funds are not limited, NPV is preferred: it measures the absolute value added. IRR is a percentage and favours small projects, and it assumes cash is reinvested at the IRR.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q14; BEE 2024 P1 L3","order":1318},
{"id":"p1-067","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What do these terms mean: net operating cash inflow, economic life, salvage value, depreciation?","back":"Net operating cash inflow: annual savings minus operating and maintenance costs (after tax where applicable). Economic life: the period over which the project gives useful savings. Salvage value: what the asset can be sold for at the end of its life (a cash inflow in the final year). Depreciation: the decrease in an asset's value over time.","explain":"Depreciation is not a cash outflow, but it lowers taxable profit and so affects after-tax cash flows. Include salvage in the last year's cash flow and any mid-life reinvestment in the year it happens.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 L3; BEE 2025 P1 Q15","order":1319},
{"id":"p1-068","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What is sensitivity analysis in project financial evaluation?","back":"Testing how NPV, IRR or payback change when key assumptions change: energy price, fuel cost, savings achieved, capital cost, project life, discount rate, or tax rate (a macro factor).","explain":"It shows which variables the project's viability depends on most, and how robust the decision is. Energy price escalation and the level of savings achieved are usually the most influential variables in energy projects.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q31","order":1320},
{"id":"p1-069","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"What are the options for financing energy efficiency projects?","back":"Debt financing (loans), equity, retained earnings (internal funds), capital lease, true (operating) lease, and performance contracting through ESCOs.","explain":"In performance contracting, the ESCO's payment is tied to the energy savings achieved, which lowers the client's risk. A stock buyback is not a financing option. Public schemes such as EEFP under NMEEE help channel finance to energy efficiency.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q48; BEE 2025 P1 Q13, L4","order":1321},
{"id":"p1-070","paper":1,"topic":"Financial management","kind":"fact","front":"How do 'guaranteed savings' and 'shared savings' ESCO contracts differ?","back":"Guaranteed savings: the client arranges the finance, and the ESCO designs and implements the project and guarantees a level of savings. Shared savings: the ESCO finances the project and is repaid from an agreed share of the savings.","explain":"In guaranteed savings, the ESCO carries the performance risk and the client carries the credit risk. In shared savings, the ESCO carries both. Measurement & Verification (M&V) checks that the guaranteed or shared savings were actually achieved.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q33, Q47","order":1322}
]$cards$::jsonb);
